1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,600
It changed the way the world eats and revolutionized the food industry.

2
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:09,080
It keeps armies alive and your beer cold.

3
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:15,820
We use it nearly 460 billion times a year to preserve everything from peaches to plutonium.

4
00:00:16,620 --> 00:00:18,360
Yet we almost never think about it.

5
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:20,540
An unsung essential of modern life.

6
00:00:21,240 --> 00:00:23,800
Now, canning on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Canning. It's the most important advance in the preservation of food in history.

8
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:52,840
There are aluminum cans for your beer, tin-plated cans for your beans.

9
00:00:53,720 --> 00:00:56,820
If you drink it or eat it, it probably comes in a can.

10
00:00:56,821 --> 00:01:03,820
Pop, chop, or peel, the technology of opening cans has changed over the years in surprising ways.

11
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:11,820
Some cans keep your food safe, while others keep you safe.

12
00:01:12,820 --> 00:01:17,820
And although the essential can design hasn't changed much over the decades, it's still evolving,

13
00:01:18,820 --> 00:01:21,820
even going so far as to cook or cool your food for you.

14
00:01:21,821 --> 00:01:27,940
you. Two of the newest technologies we're developing are ways to produce containers

15
00:01:27,941 --> 00:01:30,823
that can heat and cool beverages automatically.

16
00:01:30,824 --> 00:01:33,400
In the first case, a self-heating can uses

17
00:01:33,401 --> 00:01:38,222
chemistry to heat the contents. There are two reagents inside this container.

18
00:01:38,223 --> 00:01:38,923
There's

19
00:01:38,924 --> 00:01:41,173
a little bit of lime and a little bit of water.

20
00:01:41,174 --> 00:01:43,580
To activate it, the consumer presses a button

21
00:01:43,581 --> 00:01:48,360
on the base that breaks the seal, mixing the water and the lime, which starts the reaction.

22
00:01:48,361 --> 00:01:55,760
As those two reagents mix, the consumer will slowly turn the can, just allowing some mixing to occur.

23
00:01:56,340 --> 00:02:04,720
And in three minutes, the beverage is about 70 to 80 degrees hotter, so about 150 degrees, which would be a good drinking temperature for a cup of coffee.

24
00:02:06,020 --> 00:02:09,260
This is a self-chilling container, and it uses a completely different approach.

25
00:02:10,140 --> 00:02:13,500
The can uses heat and evaporation to cool a beverage.

26
00:02:13,501 --> 00:02:20,480
A user twists the bottom of the container, which releases a desiccant, which is a water-absorbing substance.

27
00:02:22,360 --> 00:02:29,700
The outer shell of the can is filled with water, and when the desiccant mixes with it, the water evaporates, which requires heat.

28
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:33,360
That heat comes from the enclosed beverage.

29
00:02:34,720 --> 00:02:39,700
The heat is then absorbed by vinegar salt located in a heat sink chamber in the bottom of the can.

30
00:02:39,701 --> 00:02:52,476
So if this were a room temperature beverage of 70 to 75 degrees, it would cool it to about 40 degrees Fahrenheit, the temperature of a typical refrigerator, in about

31
00:02:52,477 --> 00:02:54,180
three or four minutes.

32
00:02:55,400 --> 00:02:58,336
Self-cooling cans. A clever invention indeed.

33
00:02:58,337 --> 00:03:04,860
Yet no matter how much you jazz up a can, it's one of those inventions that seems to get no respect.

34
00:03:04,861 --> 00:03:07,980
even if its impact has been monumental.

35
00:03:08,920 --> 00:03:12,820
We take the can for granted today, yet when you think about it,

36
00:03:12,900 --> 00:03:18,160
it's probably one of the great inventions of the world, like the wheel or the light bulb.

37
00:03:18,560 --> 00:03:21,620
It's a ubiquitous part of our society. Everybody has them.

38
00:03:22,080 --> 00:03:26,120
But without it, you couldn't have moved food from the fields,

39
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:31,840
preserved them, and given people the opportunity to travel and take their food supply with them.

40
00:03:34,860 --> 00:03:42,020
The process of manufacturing a food can begins with sheets of tin plate, which is steel covered with tin.

41
00:03:43,560 --> 00:03:46,560
The metal is also covered with a coat of protective enamel.

42
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:53,500
We put enamel on the outside of the can for protection on corrosion, so it'll have shelf life,

43
00:03:53,740 --> 00:04:00,960
along with enameling the inside of the can that will protect the food from the container and the container from the food.

44
00:04:00,961 --> 00:04:07,700
The strength of the enamel required varies depending on the level of acidity in the food to be canned.

45
00:04:10,960 --> 00:04:15,600
A can of green beans doesn't need much enamel because green beans are low in acid.

46
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:21,180
But a can of tomato sauce needs thick enamel.

47
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:27,340
Because tomatoes are so acidic, they could eat through unprotected metal within a few months.

48
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:33,780
The enameled sheets of metal are first fed into a slitting machine.

49
00:04:34,760 --> 00:04:40,140
The slitter cuts those sheets into blanks for the roll forming of the body.

50
00:04:42,560 --> 00:04:52,520
Those bodies are stacked up, put into a machine, rolled into cylinders, and then welded down the side to weld the steel together.

51
00:04:52,521 --> 00:05:02,560
Once we've welded the can, it goes through a side-stripe system that puts a powder enamel over the area that we welded the can.

52
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:06,060
That protects the weld from the product and the product from the weld.

53
00:05:08,600 --> 00:05:13,180
Next, the cans are conveyed to a dual-purpose machine called a beater flanger.

54
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:20,960
At that point, we put a flange on it, which is commonly referred to in layman's terms as a lip.

55
00:05:20,961 --> 00:05:27,960
That allows us to put the double seam on, which is how the end is attached to the can.

56
00:05:27,961 --> 00:05:31,960
The same machine also puts beads on the can.

57
00:05:31,961 --> 00:05:36,960
The accordion-like beads allow the can to stretch and expand under high temperatures.

58
00:05:36,961 --> 00:05:40,960
That gives the can strength from the standpoint of vacuum.

59
00:05:40,961 --> 00:05:47,960
The beads on the end of the can give it strength from the process of cooking, which builds pressure.

60
00:05:47,961 --> 00:05:54,640
Once you've sealed this can and put product in it and put it in the cooker, it swells and the ends actually push outward.

61
00:05:54,860 --> 00:06:00,700
If those beads weren't on the ends, you would buckle the end, which would break down the integrity of the double seam.

62
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:05,320
Next, an end is attached to the can at the seamer.

63
00:06:06,060 --> 00:06:08,980
The other end will be attached when the can is filled with a product.

64
00:06:10,760 --> 00:06:15,360
From the seamer, we test the integrity of the can through an air tester.

65
00:06:16,180 --> 00:06:21,340
We seal the can at both ends and put a small amount of air in it and look to see if it leaks.

66
00:06:22,160 --> 00:06:31,600
If we would find that the end of the can, the double seam, the weld, or the steel itself has any imperfections in it, the can would be discarded.

67
00:06:35,300 --> 00:06:38,700
Once the can is tested, it's sent to be palletized.

68
00:06:38,701 --> 00:06:51,700
This factory produces 3 million cans a day, approximately 1 billion cans a year.

69
00:06:51,701 --> 00:06:55,700
During the produce season, the cans are delivered to a canning company.

70
00:06:55,701 --> 00:07:02,700
In this case, the Del Monte plant in Crystal City, Texas, where they're canning string beans.

71
00:07:02,701 --> 00:07:07,700
For centuries, the basic principle of canning has remained the same.

72
00:07:07,701 --> 00:07:14,660
Food is sealed in airtight containers and then cooked to temperatures usually between 240 and 250 degrees Fahrenheit

73
00:07:14,661 --> 00:07:18,840
in order to destroy any contaminated microorganisms it might contain.

74
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:25,540
When we bring beans in from the field, we'll go through numerous steps in our preparation process.

75
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:30,360
First, we'll air clean them to get out any loose particulate matter.

76
00:07:30,361 --> 00:07:37,700
We'll go to a washing method to remove any grit, any small pieces that we don't want on the beans.

77
00:07:39,560 --> 00:07:41,680
Then we go and we snip the beans.

78
00:07:42,080 --> 00:07:47,520
We run them through a series of rotating drums that snip off the ends, so you don't have to do that at home.

79
00:07:50,720 --> 00:07:56,000
Then the beans are brought inside the plant, where they're run through a computerized vision sorting system.

80
00:07:57,420 --> 00:07:59,000
It's a color camera system.

81
00:07:59,001 --> 00:08:03,700
The cameras take online pictures of the product passing through this machine.

82
00:08:04,220 --> 00:08:08,820
The computer analyzes the picture, determines if that is a bean or not,

83
00:08:09,220 --> 00:08:11,800
and it rejects the items that it doesn't deem suitable.

84
00:08:13,100 --> 00:08:14,920
And it rejects them of a blast of air.

85
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:19,200
Next, the beans are separated by size.

86
00:08:19,860 --> 00:08:24,300
The larger beans are canned in the French style, and the smaller ones as cut beans.

87
00:08:24,301 --> 00:08:32,640
Then they're blanched at approximately 170 degrees Fahrenheit.

88
00:08:34,140 --> 00:08:39,880
This process locks the color of the beans and also drives out any air that might still be trapped inside them.

89
00:08:41,640 --> 00:08:46,320
It's all important to lose this air in order to maintain a vacuum when the can is sealed.

90
00:08:49,780 --> 00:08:51,620
Then the beans are put into the cans.

91
00:08:51,621 --> 00:08:55,720
and they're shook and the beans are deposited down into the can

92
00:08:55,721 --> 00:08:58,000
so we make sure we have a proper fill into the can.

93
00:08:58,580 --> 00:09:02,900
From the filler, we next go to what we call our briner or add water system.

94
00:09:03,460 --> 00:09:07,180
And the briner is a hot water with a slight solution of salt.

95
00:09:07,780 --> 00:09:11,840
And what we have there is the water drives out any of the other air in the can itself.

96
00:09:13,100 --> 00:09:14,880
The salt, that's added for taste.

97
00:09:16,340 --> 00:09:18,220
Next, the cans move to the topper.

98
00:09:18,820 --> 00:09:20,480
The topper is a rotating disc cup.

99
00:09:20,481 --> 00:09:25,440
and basically it comes in and it pushes out any excess water to make sure that we have a proper fill.

100
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:27,740
We want to have the proper headspace as we're operating.

101
00:09:30,040 --> 00:09:32,740
Headspace is extremely important in the canning process.

102
00:09:33,840 --> 00:09:37,780
It's the amount of distance between the top of the product and the bottom of the lid.

103
00:09:39,100 --> 00:09:41,880
Too little headspace causes cans to bulge during heating.

104
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:45,260
Too much headspace results in underweight cans.

105
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,920
The seamer is the machine that attaches the lids.

106
00:09:50,480 --> 00:09:56,580
Right before this is done, a nozzle blows a shot of steam into the headspace area, displacing

107
00:09:56,581 --> 00:09:59,680
any air.

108
00:09:59,681 --> 00:10:04,520
This is a crucial moment in the process, because part of the magic of canning lies in its ability

109
00:10:04,521 --> 00:10:10,520
to protect food from the decay that occurs in the presence of air.

110
00:10:10,521 --> 00:10:14,920
Now with the lid attached, the can has become an airtight vacuum.

111
00:10:14,921 --> 00:10:20,880
Next, a shelf life code is applied, indicating what day the product was canned and by what

112
00:10:20,881 --> 00:10:23,280
date it should be consumed.

113
00:10:23,281 --> 00:10:31,100
Although some canned foods can last much longer than the recommended consumption date.

114
00:10:31,101 --> 00:10:36,640
The cans are then taken to a giant cooking vessel known as a retort.

115
00:10:36,641 --> 00:10:39,040
What we do is we load the retort up with our crates.

116
00:10:39,041 --> 00:10:42,000
We can maximize seven crates in every retort.

117
00:10:42,001 --> 00:10:45,940
Each batch, after the door is shut, has to have the air expelled from the vessel to ensure

118
00:10:45,941 --> 00:10:49,700
we have a proper steam environment for cooking.

119
00:10:49,701 --> 00:10:55,640
The control system gives us a proper temperature and time of cook, which is all governed by

120
00:10:55,641 --> 00:10:59,440
the computer program.

121
00:10:59,441 --> 00:11:03,960
The food is cooked inside the cans rather than prior to being canned, because this method

122
00:11:03,961 --> 00:11:07,360
creates the most sterile environment.

123
00:11:07,361 --> 00:11:13,080
No new bacteria can form in the food once the can has been sealed.

124
00:11:13,081 --> 00:11:18,420
The ingredients will now stay fresh until the can is opened.

125
00:11:18,421 --> 00:11:24,440
They're labeled, packaged, and sent off.

126
00:11:24,441 --> 00:11:32,680
This high-tech canning process is a far cry from the first experiments with food preservation.

127
00:11:32,681 --> 00:11:35,700
civilization suspected that air was the enemy of food and wine.

128
00:11:37,980 --> 00:11:43,220
Over 3,000 years ago, the Greeks sealed their wine in airtight vessels using pine resin.

129
00:11:44,900 --> 00:11:49,820
By the end of the 17th century, winemakers began to stop their bottles with corks.

130
00:11:51,080 --> 00:11:55,960
Food was protected in different ways, by pouring hot oil over it or by coating it with melted

131
00:11:55,961 --> 00:11:56,661
fat.

132
00:11:56,662 --> 00:12:03,300
Many foods were preserved with salt, but that approach drained important nutrients

133
00:12:03,301 --> 00:12:04,001
and taste.

134
00:12:04,002 --> 00:12:08,660
None of these several methods could truly protect food from the deleterious effects

135
00:12:08,661 --> 00:12:09,361
of air.

136
00:12:09,362 --> 00:12:15,660
The world was looking for a clean, long-lasting preservation method that wouldn't

137
00:12:15,661 --> 00:12:18,377
rob food of critical nutrients.

138
00:12:18,378 --> 00:12:23,460
Surprisingly, the man who kicked off the Cannes Revolution

139
00:12:23,461 --> 00:12:25,380
was Napoleon Bonaparte,

140
00:12:25,820 --> 00:12:27,280
who was famous for saying,

141
00:12:27,800 --> 00:12:29,300
an army travels on its stomach.

142
00:12:31,700 --> 00:12:33,500
Napoleon was tired of supplying his troops

143
00:12:33,501 --> 00:12:35,000
with food foraged in the field.

144
00:12:35,840 --> 00:12:38,460
In 1795, he urged the French government

145
00:12:38,461 --> 00:12:40,700
to offer a 12,000 franc prize

146
00:12:40,701 --> 00:12:42,860
to any inventor who could devise a method

147
00:12:42,861 --> 00:12:44,220
of preserving fresh food

148
00:12:44,221 --> 00:12:46,020
and transporting it to his army.

149
00:12:49,260 --> 00:12:52,780
In 1809, a Parisian named Nicolas Appare

150
00:12:52,781 --> 00:12:54,680
came up with the idea for canning.

151
00:12:56,520 --> 00:12:57,760
A former vintner,

152
00:12:58,320 --> 00:13:00,000
Appare assumed that just like wine,

153
00:13:00,340 --> 00:13:02,000
food spoiled when exposed to air.

154
00:13:04,460 --> 00:13:06,000
After years of experimenting,

155
00:13:06,520 --> 00:13:07,920
he found that boiling foods,

156
00:13:08,140 --> 00:13:09,420
such as poultry and fruits,

157
00:13:09,680 --> 00:13:10,740
in airtight bottles,

158
00:13:11,260 --> 00:13:12,400
could keep the food fresh.

159
00:13:14,860 --> 00:13:16,340
Napoleon was sold on the idea

160
00:13:16,341 --> 00:13:18,960
and handed Appare the prize money himself.

161
00:13:18,961 --> 00:13:25,960
Soon after, in 1810, Englishman Peter Durand adapted a pair's idea.

162
00:13:25,961 --> 00:13:29,960
He replaced the breakable bottles with tin plate cans,

163
00:13:29,961 --> 00:13:31,960
which were iron cans coated with tin.

164
00:13:31,961 --> 00:13:36,960
The tin served to protect the iron because it rusts and corrodes much more slowly than iron.

165
00:13:36,961 --> 00:13:39,960
The cans were airtight.

166
00:13:39,961 --> 00:13:44,960
Now with cans, fresh food could be available everywhere, at any time.

167
00:13:44,961 --> 00:13:47,960
everywhere, at any time.

168
00:13:47,961 --> 00:13:51,300
The new invention changed the way the world ate.

169
00:13:51,301 --> 00:13:55,960
By putting it in a can, sealing it, heating it properly,

170
00:13:55,961 --> 00:14:00,000
you could move nutritious, high-quality food

171
00:14:00,001 --> 00:14:01,460
anywhere in the world.

172
00:14:01,461 --> 00:14:04,620
The British Navy was using over 40,000 pounds

173
00:14:04,621 --> 00:14:08,620
of canned food a year by 1813, because it

174
00:14:08,621 --> 00:14:11,480
helped eliminate scurvy and other shipboard diseases

175
00:14:11,481 --> 00:14:16,160
that were basically because of lack of vitamins and nutrients in the food supply

176
00:14:16,161 --> 00:14:18,560
for the sailors at sea for long periods of time.

177
00:14:19,820 --> 00:14:23,000
Canned goods quickly spread across the Atlantic to America,

178
00:14:23,540 --> 00:14:27,820
where they helped feed pioneers as they trekked across the continent to settle the West.

179
00:14:30,780 --> 00:14:33,320
Gold rush miners survived on canned beans,

180
00:14:33,680 --> 00:14:37,660
and during the Civil War, canned food was deemed almost as important

181
00:14:37,661 --> 00:14:40,020
for keeping soldiers alive as their weapons.

182
00:14:41,480 --> 00:14:46,200
Yet making the cans themselves was labor-intensive in the middle of the 19th century.

183
00:14:47,320 --> 00:14:51,640
Each can was handmade, with a workman hammering and soldering it into shape.

184
00:14:52,700 --> 00:14:56,460
A skilled laborer could usually only make from six to ten cans a day.

185
00:14:58,200 --> 00:15:02,380
But this was all about to change with the advent of mass production.

186
00:15:03,700 --> 00:15:05,960
Soon millions of cans would be produced per year.

187
00:15:06,760 --> 00:15:10,420
During the 20th century, amazing new designs would be developed.

188
00:15:10,421 --> 00:15:13,180
It would take canning convenience to another level.

189
00:15:14,660 --> 00:15:18,980
Yet one kind of can would actually threaten the very atmosphere of our planet.

190
00:15:22,860 --> 00:15:26,860
Most canned food has a suggested shelf life of at least two years.

191
00:15:27,420 --> 00:15:29,820
Yet it can remain preserved for much longer.

192
00:15:30,760 --> 00:15:37,080
Recently, a man in England opened and ate a 50-year-old can of chicken with no ill effects.

193
00:15:37,081 --> 00:15:40,980
Canning will return on Modern Marvels.

194
00:15:42,480 --> 00:15:45,800
We now return to Canning on Modern Marvels.

195
00:15:52,320 --> 00:15:56,660
Americans each use an average of over 140 food cans a year.

196
00:15:57,620 --> 00:16:02,540
But whether it's tuna, vegetables, or jams, the canning process is essentially the same.

197
00:16:03,200 --> 00:16:05,900
Food is sealed in airtight containers and then cooked.

198
00:16:07,080 --> 00:16:11,120
Yet all of these canned foods have a problem when it comes to microwave ovens.

199
00:16:11,860 --> 00:16:13,100
The can itself.

200
00:16:15,940 --> 00:16:19,220
There's a common misperception that metal can't be used in the microwave.

201
00:16:19,640 --> 00:16:22,760
That's actually untrue if you design the container properly.

202
00:16:23,540 --> 00:16:26,360
So, for example, this is a traditional metal can.

203
00:16:26,640 --> 00:16:28,580
And in fact, it won't work in the microwave.

204
00:16:29,240 --> 00:16:32,800
If I were to put it in and turn it on,

205
00:16:33,120 --> 00:16:36,480
all of the microwave energy would be reflected off of the can.

206
00:16:36,481 --> 00:16:40,100
So none of the energy would get into the contents of the food.

207
00:16:40,560 --> 00:16:44,200
If you design the container properly, such as this,

208
00:16:44,500 --> 00:16:48,260
so that you have a shallow metal container with a wide opening,

209
00:16:48,780 --> 00:16:52,100
because of that, the energy will be absorbed through the opening,

210
00:16:52,420 --> 00:16:54,140
then it will work perfectly in the microwave.

211
00:16:54,620 --> 00:16:58,660
So the consumer lifts the tab and removes the peelable end

212
00:16:58,661 --> 00:17:02,220
and places it in the microwave and turns it on.

213
00:17:06,480 --> 00:17:11,240
So as you can see, the product is bubbling.

214
00:17:11,241 --> 00:17:16,720
It's been heated like it were in an ordinary container.

215
00:17:16,721 --> 00:17:21,160
Microwave cans are just the latest in a long line of innovations developed by food can

216
00:17:21,161 --> 00:17:23,840
manufacturers.

217
00:17:23,841 --> 00:17:28,420
One of the biggest leaps in progress came in the late 19th century, with the introduction

218
00:17:28,421 --> 00:17:31,680
of mass production to the business.

219
00:17:31,681 --> 00:17:36,840
Assembly lines and mechanical body makers allowed manufacturers to produce thousands of cans per day.

220
00:17:38,120 --> 00:17:40,340
And these new cans were also safer.

221
00:17:41,820 --> 00:17:47,020
And in early cans, where they were soldered together, the tendency for leakage was much higher.

222
00:17:47,600 --> 00:17:50,480
The solder wouldn't withstand transport very easily.

223
00:17:51,080 --> 00:17:53,400
The cans would break apart.

224
00:17:54,040 --> 00:17:55,460
The metal would corrode.

225
00:17:55,940 --> 00:18:01,400
As we improved that process and the consistency of the metal, a lot of that declined.

226
00:18:01,680 --> 00:18:12,000
People were able to accept the can as a standard of quality and a standard of delivery that was going to be safe from things like botulism, which will kill you.

227
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:16,700
Botulism is a bacteria that grows in oxygen in a can.

228
00:18:17,180 --> 00:18:26,900
Your mother may have told you, don't buy dented cans at the supermarket because of the concern that if the can was dented, it might have some oxygen leaking into it

229
00:18:26,901 --> 00:18:30,160
and therefore the contents of the can would be spoiled.

230
00:18:31,680 --> 00:18:37,480
During the World Wars, cans and canning were responsible for keeping millions of soldiers fed.

231
00:18:38,180 --> 00:18:45,220
During World War II, the Army was looking for a way to feed a soldier on the move very rapidly.

232
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:51,700
And they created packaged food in cans called C-rations and K-rations.

233
00:18:52,240 --> 00:18:53,420
Everything was in cans.

234
00:18:53,880 --> 00:18:58,420
When you got a C-ration, you got a little box with a couple of cans in it.

235
00:18:58,421 --> 00:19:01,620
One can would be soup or stew.

236
00:19:02,120 --> 00:19:04,980
Inside another one would be crackers, biscuits and cheese.

237
00:19:07,000 --> 00:19:09,980
The food can really caught on after World War II,

238
00:19:10,480 --> 00:19:12,680
when people began craving it for its efficiency.

239
00:19:13,980 --> 00:19:16,940
Now, from tuna to carrots to fruit cocktail,

240
00:19:17,360 --> 00:19:18,820
it was all about the can.

241
00:19:21,320 --> 00:19:23,220
But it's not all about the food.

242
00:19:24,240 --> 00:19:27,200
You'll need canned cold ones to wash down your canned meal.

243
00:19:28,420 --> 00:19:32,580
The use of cans for beverages now greatly surpasses the use of cans for food.

244
00:19:34,660 --> 00:19:39,620
The first successful beverage cans were introduced in 1935 by the Kruger Brewing Company.

245
00:19:40,660 --> 00:19:44,500
The containers were developed only after steel cans became strong enough to accommodate the

246
00:19:44,501 --> 00:19:49,380
internal pressure created when the beer was pasteurized and when effective coatings were

247
00:19:49,381 --> 00:19:52,260
introduced that eliminated any metallic taste from the can.

248
00:19:52,261 --> 00:20:04,020
The first aluminum beverage can was manufactured in 1963.

249
00:20:04,021 --> 00:20:08,760
Aluminum is easily molded, lightweight, and resistant to corrosion.

250
00:20:08,761 --> 00:20:15,020
By 1985, aluminum cans were the most popular beverage containers in any market.

251
00:20:15,021 --> 00:20:21,760
Today consumers buy soft drinks in aluminum cans four times as often as in plastic bottles.

252
00:20:21,761 --> 00:20:25,220
Recently, the aluminum can morphed into a user-pleasing hybrid.

253
00:20:26,140 --> 00:20:28,160
This is one of the latest in packaging innovations.

254
00:20:28,280 --> 00:20:29,300
It's called a bottle can.

255
00:20:29,800 --> 00:20:33,100
It's made of aluminum, but it's actually shaped like a bottle.

256
00:20:33,600 --> 00:20:42,100
Consumers really love this because of the great graphics, its sleek look, its chillability, and its chill feel, and doesn't break like glass.

257
00:20:42,101 --> 00:20:46,340
It's been very popular with those in the beer and soft drink industry.

258
00:20:46,341 --> 00:20:53,700
Another can that's already gotten its makeover is the aerosol can.

259
00:20:53,701 --> 00:21:00,040
Patented in 1926 by Erik Rothheim, a Norwegian chemical engineer, aerosol came into its own

260
00:21:00,041 --> 00:21:04,840
during World War II when the American military was looking for an effective way to deal with

261
00:21:04,841 --> 00:21:09,500
insects during jungle warfare.

262
00:21:09,501 --> 00:21:14,900
Before the invention of the aerosol can and the use of spray insecticides, the primary

263
00:21:14,901 --> 00:21:16,655
tool was a pump spray.

264
00:21:16,656 --> 00:21:21,680
It had a container that had the insecticide in it and a big air

265
00:21:21,681 --> 00:21:24,423
valve on top that one would pump through.

266
00:21:24,424 --> 00:21:27,300
Very, very impractical for a soldier trying

267
00:21:27,301 --> 00:21:30,800
to crawl through the jungle to pick up his pump sprangle.

268
00:21:33,600 --> 00:21:35,950
The aerosol can was just the ticket.

269
00:21:35,951 --> 00:21:39,280
Small, lightweight, and convenient, a soldier could

270
00:21:39,281 --> 00:21:44,560
spray insecticides with the push of a button.

271
00:21:44,561 --> 00:21:48,520
Aerosol cans worked with the use of a propellant.

272
00:21:48,521 --> 00:21:51,940
The propellant is a pressurized gas.

273
00:21:51,941 --> 00:21:55,520
The nozzle at the top of the can opens when depressed.

274
00:21:55,521 --> 00:21:58,280
Then the force of the gas pushing down on the product

275
00:21:58,281 --> 00:22:03,060
moves it through the dip tube up to the nozzle.

276
00:22:03,061 --> 00:22:06,440
The narrow nozzle atomizes the liquid and forms a fine mist.

277
00:22:09,280 --> 00:22:16,280
Early aerosol cans used chlorofluorocarbons, or CFCs, as propellants.

278
00:22:16,281 --> 00:22:22,680
Scientists discovered that once these chemicals were sprayed out of the cans, they drifted

279
00:22:22,681 --> 00:22:25,000
upwards into the atmosphere.

280
00:22:25,001 --> 00:22:31,360
At high altitudes, the chlorine in the CFC molecules began to break down the ozone layer

281
00:22:31,361 --> 00:22:34,820
that surrounds the Earth.

282
00:22:34,821 --> 00:22:37,640
Ozone is a gas, a form of oxygen.

283
00:22:37,641 --> 00:22:42,040
It serves to protect the Earth from the sun's powerful rays.

284
00:22:42,041 --> 00:22:48,400
Without it, we can count on more skin cancer and global warming.

285
00:22:48,401 --> 00:22:54,904
The CFC propellants were removed from more than 90% of all aerosols in the U.S.

286
00:22:54,905 --> 00:22:55,605
by 1978

287
00:22:55,606 --> 00:23:01,800
and replaced by other propellants such as isobutane and dimethyl ether.

288
00:23:01,801 --> 00:23:06,000
That's helped rebuild the ozone layer.

289
00:23:06,001 --> 00:23:13,000
However, some scientists believe the ozone layer won't return to pre-1980 levels until around 2070.

290
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:19,000
Current aerosol cans may no longer be a major threat to the atmosphere.

291
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,333
But be careful of how you dispose of them.

292
00:23:22,334 --> 00:23:25,000
If they're thrown into a fire, they can explode.

293
00:23:29,000 --> 00:23:34,000
Microwavable and aerosol cans represent technological leaps into our brave new world.

294
00:23:34,001 --> 00:23:41,280
But for generations, it's been a lower-tech can that's been found in practically every household where someone has a garden.

295
00:23:43,060 --> 00:23:46,480
Home canning has been a household tradition for nearly 200 years.

296
00:23:47,940 --> 00:23:51,060
There are two types of processes used for home canning.

297
00:23:51,920 --> 00:23:56,540
We have the boiling water canning process, which is used with only high-acid foods.

298
00:23:58,400 --> 00:24:03,540
Jams, jellies, any type of soft spread like a conserved marmalade also includes tomatoes.

299
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:08,960
and the steam pressure canner process which is used with only low acid foods.

300
00:24:08,961 --> 00:24:13,600
Things like vegetables, so your carrots, green beans, anything of that category.

301
00:24:13,601 --> 00:24:19,700
A steam pressure canner is necessary because it's capable of reaching the higher temperatures needed

302
00:24:19,701 --> 00:24:23,200
to kill off the microorganisms found in low acid foods.

303
00:24:23,201 --> 00:24:26,700
The result? Tasty food that'll last for years.

304
00:24:26,701 --> 00:24:32,000
From home canning fare

305
00:24:32,001 --> 00:24:34,000
to factory canned products.

306
00:24:34,001 --> 00:24:37,000
All forms of cans breed a necessary counterpart.

307
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
Can openers.

308
00:24:40,001 --> 00:24:43,000
From the primitive to the powerful.

309
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:47,000
The simple to the complex.

310
00:24:47,001 --> 00:24:49,000
There's one for every task.

311
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:53,000
Even special openers that keep hardcore prisoners

312
00:24:53,001 --> 00:24:56,000
from opening up each other.

313
00:24:56,001 --> 00:25:01,940
We won't be running out of aluminum for cans anytime soon.

314
00:25:02,620 --> 00:25:05,820
It is the most abundant metal in the Earth's crust.

315
00:25:06,940 --> 00:25:11,540
Canning will return on Modern Marvels here on the History Channel.

316
00:25:13,900 --> 00:25:17,100
We now return to Canning on Modern Marvels.

317
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:30,660
It took humans hundreds of years to devise ways to put things into cans.

318
00:25:33,120 --> 00:25:35,620
And we're still working on ways to get them out again.

319
00:25:36,980 --> 00:25:39,140
Meet the titan of all can openers.

320
00:25:39,960 --> 00:25:42,340
Edlin Company's Model 825A.

321
00:25:43,180 --> 00:25:45,600
It's the most advanced can opening system in the world.

322
00:25:46,020 --> 00:25:50,000
It can be operated by one user through its computer-controlled touchscreen interface.

323
00:25:50,001 --> 00:25:57,620
The main market for the 825A can opening system is large food processing companies,

324
00:25:57,760 --> 00:26:02,900
companies that are opening hundreds and hundreds of cans a day to make pasta sauces or pizzas.

325
00:26:03,320 --> 00:26:08,000
It can open up to 30 cans a minute or 1,800 cans an hour with just one operator.

326
00:26:09,700 --> 00:26:14,040
Some companies use it to open as many as 14,000 cans per day.

327
00:26:15,300 --> 00:26:18,040
The Colossus is monitored by computer at all times.

328
00:26:18,041 --> 00:26:23,440
The operator loads the cans into the machine through the infeed conveyor.

329
00:26:23,441 --> 00:26:27,860
The infeed conveyor is a belt-driven conveyor with a rinse jet, which rinses the outside

330
00:26:27,861 --> 00:26:31,800
of the can and blows the water off the indentation at the top of the can.

331
00:26:31,801 --> 00:26:36,420
From the infeed conveyor, the can then enters the crown punch opener.

332
00:26:36,421 --> 00:26:41,220
The crown punch opener punches the top out of the can in one motion, and an alligator

333
00:26:41,221 --> 00:26:47,360
jaw comes in and removes the lid and deposits it behind the machine in a receptacle.

334
00:26:47,361 --> 00:26:54,460
The entire machine is equipped with sensors that time every move down to a millisecond, including the dumping.

335
00:26:55,160 --> 00:27:00,220
The can is then pushed through a dumping cage, which takes the can and inverts it upside down,

336
00:27:00,620 --> 00:27:04,820
pouring the product into the collection tross and into a pumping station.

337
00:27:05,520 --> 00:27:12,040
The can continues through the dumping frame, inverting completely, and is then pushed down through a rinse jet.

338
00:27:12,041 --> 00:27:19,080
The rinse jet rinses out the inside of the can clean, and the can is then pushed into a can crusher.

339
00:27:21,260 --> 00:27:24,720
All of this time-saving technology comes at a hefty price.

340
00:27:25,740 --> 00:27:32,560
Depending upon the options the customer wants, a simple, straightforward, right-line machine would cost about $65,000.

341
00:27:33,440 --> 00:27:39,520
Anywhere up with the options added, depending upon the processor's needs, to $90,000 or $100,000.

342
00:27:42,040 --> 00:27:46,940
Over the years, much research and technology has gone into making cans airtight and strong.

343
00:27:47,500 --> 00:27:49,100
But there was a problem from the start.

344
00:27:49,840 --> 00:27:51,100
How to open the darn things?

345
00:27:52,180 --> 00:27:55,940
During the 19th century, little thought had been given to can openers.

346
00:27:57,240 --> 00:28:01,720
Soldiers during the Civil War often had to use their bayonets to pry open canned food.

347
00:28:03,120 --> 00:28:08,900
The first patented can opener was invented by Ezra Warner of Waterbury, Connecticut in 1858.

348
00:28:08,901 --> 00:28:17,840
The modern can opener with a cutting wheel that rolls around the rim was invented by American William Lyman in 1870.

349
00:28:20,660 --> 00:28:25,520
During World War II, soldiers went into battle with a can opener as part of their standard gear.

350
00:28:27,020 --> 00:28:33,020
It was called the P-38 because it required exactly 38 punctures to open the can.

351
00:28:33,021 --> 00:28:39,520
Consumers opened most beverages with so-called church keys, at least until 1966.

352
00:28:40,940 --> 00:28:43,360
That was the year pull tops were introduced.

353
00:28:43,980 --> 00:28:49,180
Pull tabs worked very well, but there was a lot of controversy around them because there was a lot of litter,

354
00:28:49,780 --> 00:28:53,680
and there was always concern that someone was going to get cut on the edge or something like that.

355
00:28:54,200 --> 00:29:00,540
And the new Easy Open Tab can, which pushes the top back into the can, was invented.

356
00:29:01,040 --> 00:29:06,560
Easy open tabs are great for beverages, but today pop open tops rule the world of food cans.

357
00:29:07,100 --> 00:29:10,420
Now they're being rivaled by a new generation, the peel open top.

358
00:29:10,960 --> 00:29:15,020
One of the latest technologies that's been developed is this new peel seam technology.

359
00:29:15,560 --> 00:29:18,800
So this is a flexible lid. It's still a metal barrier,

360
00:29:18,940 --> 00:29:23,560
so you still have the oxygen and light protection that you've come to expect from metal cans.

361
00:29:23,561 --> 00:29:29,040
But now we're able to open it with perhaps half the opening force of a traditional end.

362
00:29:30,540 --> 00:29:35,040
Despite all of these innovations, there's still plenty of need for can openers.

363
00:29:36,540 --> 00:29:40,100
One of the most unusual openers has been designed for use in prisons,

364
00:29:40,580 --> 00:29:45,120
where convicts often work in the kitchens and might steal a can lid to use as a weapon.

365
00:29:46,580 --> 00:29:51,880
Using one prison as an example, it's a high population, 20,000 or so transient prisoners,

366
00:29:52,440 --> 00:29:57,880
with four different food service facilities, and they're opening cans three times a day.

367
00:29:57,881 --> 00:30:07,256
If one lid goes missing, they have to do a lockdown of the prison and call in a SWAT team just to be able to find that potential weapon that can be very dangerous in

368
00:30:07,257 --> 00:30:08,620
that type of population.

369
00:30:09,540 --> 00:30:12,600
This is our Model 625 APP can opening system.

370
00:30:12,920 --> 00:30:15,180
It was designed for a large prison in New York City.

371
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:23,920
The idea is that it delids the can, takes the lid away, and securely stores it in a receptacle so that the prisoners don't have access to the lid to use as a weapon.

372
00:30:24,920 --> 00:30:29,440
The unit comes equipped with a can counter and a lid counter.

373
00:30:29,720 --> 00:30:33,580
The can counter counts up the number of cans opened, and the lid counter counts down.

374
00:30:33,960 --> 00:30:37,260
These two counters have to match, or the machine shuts down and won't operate.

375
00:30:37,440 --> 00:30:39,740
That alerts the security officer that there's a problem.

376
00:30:40,560 --> 00:30:45,180
To set up the machine, you need to insert the security key and turn it to the operating position.

377
00:30:45,820 --> 00:30:50,280
To operate the machine, you insert a can into the unit and depress the two green buttons.

378
00:30:53,920 --> 00:30:58,460
The green light indicates to the operator when the machine has completed a full cycle.

379
00:31:00,560 --> 00:31:04,560
With the machine's protective shell removed, we can see its inner workings.

380
00:31:05,440 --> 00:31:08,020
The lids are stored in a secure area.

381
00:31:08,640 --> 00:31:16,600
The security officer has a key which unlocks the padlock and gives him access to the lid receptacle.

382
00:31:17,900 --> 00:31:20,680
Used lids and cans are more than a security risk.

383
00:31:21,660 --> 00:31:24,900
They've also created massive amounts of landfill waste.

384
00:31:26,340 --> 00:31:32,920
Yet that set the stage for an effort that resulted in the savings of billions of dollars a year in electricity.

385
00:31:36,680 --> 00:31:42,100
The ideal shelf life of many canned beers is no longer than 120 days.

386
00:31:42,960 --> 00:31:49,240
However, such factors as pasteurization, refrigeration, and alcohol content can extend the shelf life.

387
00:31:49,241 --> 00:31:53,380
Canning will return on Modern Marvels.

388
00:31:56,200 --> 00:31:59,480
We now return to Canning on Modern Marvels.

389
00:32:03,780 --> 00:32:07,380
The phenomenal success of the can came at a huge price.

390
00:32:08,220 --> 00:32:11,420
Millions of pounds of wasted metal dumped into landfills.

391
00:32:11,421 --> 00:32:20,140
In the 1970s, a recycling revolution took place that led to the can becoming the most recycled of all food containers.

392
00:32:21,260 --> 00:32:24,100
Can recycling is the most successful environmental story.

393
00:32:25,100 --> 00:32:28,600
Aluminum cans and steel cans are recycled at a 50% rate or higher.

394
00:32:31,800 --> 00:32:36,900
In the United States and elsewhere, billions of aluminum cans are collected and recycled.

395
00:32:36,901 --> 00:32:45,100
It only takes 5% of the energy to recycle a can that it would take to make a new can from raw metal.

396
00:32:45,880 --> 00:32:51,880
As a matter of fact, recycling is so prominent that the aluminum beverage can that you use today

397
00:32:51,881 --> 00:32:56,180
will be made into six new beverage cans before this year is out.

398
00:32:56,800 --> 00:32:58,320
So it's recycled constantly.

399
00:33:00,120 --> 00:33:04,080
Anheuser-Busch runs one of the biggest and most successful recycling operations.

400
00:33:04,081 --> 00:33:09,900
Consumers have several different options of how they want to recycle their aluminum beverage cans.

401
00:33:10,020 --> 00:33:13,680
Some of them have curbside programs. Others have voluntary drop-off facilities.

402
00:33:14,460 --> 00:33:15,760
Others go to buyback centers.

403
00:33:19,460 --> 00:33:22,960
At the buyback center, the cans are immediately weighed in bulk.

404
00:33:23,820 --> 00:33:28,300
Once they hit the scale, that's what determines what is going to be paid to the consumer.

405
00:33:28,301 --> 00:33:34,520
The price varies based on what the individual buyback center is receiving for their metal.

406
00:33:36,600 --> 00:33:40,460
Some recycling centers process tens of millions of cans a year.

407
00:33:41,800 --> 00:33:47,820
Nationwide, approximately 113,000 aluminum cans are recycled every minute of every day.

408
00:33:48,960 --> 00:33:52,000
They are then put through what we call a baler.

409
00:33:52,001 --> 00:34:01,880
And what a baler does is it compacts approximately 700 to almost 1,000 pounds, depending on the baler, of cans into a bale.

410
00:34:03,500 --> 00:34:07,120
Those bales are then put into typically trucks, sometimes rail.

411
00:34:07,800 --> 00:34:13,300
A truck would carry about 40,000 pounds of bales, which would be about 40 large bales.

412
00:34:16,540 --> 00:34:21,560
The bales are taken to a reclamation facility where they're melted down into molten metal.

413
00:34:22,000 --> 00:34:23,720
which is cast as new aluminum.

414
00:34:25,080 --> 00:34:27,360
This aluminum is then made into new cans.

415
00:34:28,080 --> 00:34:32,340
This year, we recycled more than 27 billion aluminum cans.

416
00:34:34,360 --> 00:34:37,460
Of course, cans weren't the first metal objects to be recycled.

417
00:34:38,680 --> 00:34:40,980
Ancient Romans would gather scrap bronze

418
00:34:40,981 --> 00:34:43,480
and melt it down to be recast as weapons.

419
00:34:46,080 --> 00:34:48,620
Resource shortages caused by the world wars

420
00:34:48,621 --> 00:34:50,740
greatly increased the need for recycling.

421
00:34:51,620 --> 00:34:56,060
People were encouraged to donate everything from old automobiles to tin cans

422
00:34:56,061 --> 00:34:58,160
in order to increase the metal supply.

423
00:34:59,160 --> 00:35:04,560
But it was during the 1970s that recycling beer and soda cans became commonplace.

424
00:35:05,500 --> 00:35:08,320
As the environmental movement was taking off,

425
00:35:08,960 --> 00:35:14,860
the waste that was going into the ground in the terms of landfills was just found unacceptable.

426
00:35:14,861 --> 00:35:23,560
and recycling as a way of controlling waste became very prominent in the American mind and around the world.

427
00:35:24,140 --> 00:35:29,180
It also turned out to be a really good idea because recovering metals,

428
00:35:29,660 --> 00:35:34,780
particularly aluminum more than anything else, actually pays for most recycling.

429
00:35:34,781 --> 00:35:39,140
The aluminum can industry paid more than a billion dollars in the last year

430
00:35:39,141 --> 00:35:44,780
to consumers, municipalities, and businesses to recycle those aluminum cans.

431
00:35:44,860 --> 00:35:51,860
Another way can manufacturers are saving resources is by streamlining the cans themselves.

432
00:35:51,861 --> 00:35:53,860
They've become lighter.

433
00:35:53,861 --> 00:36:01,860
Since the 1970s, aluminum can weight has dropped from 55 pounds per thousand to 29.5 pounds per thousand.

434
00:36:01,861 --> 00:36:06,860
This is accomplished by what is called geometric optimization.

435
00:36:06,861 --> 00:36:11,860
A new super end is literally allowing can makers to cut corners.

436
00:36:11,861 --> 00:36:13,620
cut corners.

437
00:36:13,621 --> 00:36:18,980
A recent example of geometric optimization or the kind of light weighting that we can

438
00:36:18,981 --> 00:36:23,740
produce with technology is through our super end beverage technology.

439
00:36:23,741 --> 00:36:29,340
A traditional beverage end has a nearly vertical wall at this position.

440
00:36:29,341 --> 00:36:36,740
You can see the difference with this super end where the wall is about a 45 degree angle.

441
00:36:36,741 --> 00:36:40,800
Now that fairly simple sounding change has had quite an impact.

442
00:36:41,280 --> 00:36:47,680
By using a 45 degree angle, we can save over 10% of the metal in the end.

443
00:36:47,940 --> 00:36:49,700
That's a very significant savings.

444
00:36:50,120 --> 00:36:54,300
By the end of this year, we will have produced 100 billion of these ends.

445
00:36:54,301 --> 00:36:57,920
That will have saved about 25,000 tons of aluminum.

446
00:36:59,880 --> 00:37:03,140
Decades of can shaving has produced a metal saving miracle.

447
00:37:03,141 --> 00:37:07,140
This container was produced in 1971.

448
00:37:07,141 --> 00:37:12,140
Today, four containers can be produced with the same amount of material.

449
00:37:14,140 --> 00:37:18,140
Light and lighter aluminum cans may hold our beer,

450
00:37:18,141 --> 00:37:23,140
but we need real heavyweights to hold some of the most toxic substances on Earth.

451
00:37:27,140 --> 00:37:32,140
Recycling one aluminum can saves enough electricity to run a TV set for three hours.

452
00:37:33,140 --> 00:37:38,140
Canning will return on Modern Marvels.

453
00:37:38,141 --> 00:37:43,140
We now return to Canning on Modern Marvels.

454
00:37:48,140 --> 00:37:54,140
Cans are the go-to container for an incredibly wide variety of substances.

455
00:37:54,141 --> 00:38:02,140
Today, over 1,500 different types of products are preserved in over 460 billion cans per year.

456
00:38:03,140 --> 00:38:13,981
But certainly one of their most demanding jobs involves storing radioactive nuclear waste generated as a byproduct from the processing of nuclear material for national

457
00:38:13,982 --> 00:38:14,682
defense.

458
00:38:15,720 --> 00:38:20,880
The material is highly radioactive and will be for thousands of years.

459
00:38:22,020 --> 00:38:31,392
The Defense Waste Processing Facility at the United States Department of Energy's Savannah Riverside in South Carolina carries out the important process of storing

460
00:38:31,394 --> 00:38:32,600
nuclear waste safely.

461
00:38:33,140 --> 00:38:42,680
We have the full array of materials that are produced in the production of nuclear materials

462
00:38:42,681 --> 00:38:48,380
and reactors. We do have some amount of plutonium. We have technetium.

463
00:38:48,381 --> 00:38:56,380
All of the waste processed here has been generated by Savannah River site reactors.

464
00:38:56,381 --> 00:39:01,200
The first step is encasing the radioactive nuclear waste in glass.

465
00:39:01,201 --> 00:39:02,880
The procedure is called

466
00:39:02,881 --> 00:39:03,960
vitrification.

467
00:39:03,961 --> 00:39:09,440
The nuclear waste enters the vitrification process as a form of sludge.

468
00:39:09,441 --> 00:39:14,840
It comes from the dissolving spent reactor fuels, fuels that have been in nuclear reactors,

469
00:39:15,240 --> 00:39:18,860
where it's stored in underground tanks, roughly a million gallons in each tank.

470
00:39:20,480 --> 00:39:25,824
This vitrification utilizes a material called frit. This is frit.

471
00:39:25,825 --> 00:39:27,880
It looks like what it is.

472
00:39:27,881 --> 00:39:29,720
It's sand. It's a special kind of sand.

473
00:39:30,420 --> 00:39:36,260
It's a special recipe that we've had made that allows it to bond with the waste.

474
00:39:36,860 --> 00:39:43,300
And when it cools, form a glass that is inert and lasts for a long period of time.

475
00:39:44,940 --> 00:39:49,420
At Savannah River, the sludge and the frit are glassified in a melter.

476
00:39:51,520 --> 00:39:53,260
It looks like a big teapot.

477
00:39:53,960 --> 00:39:57,400
And it's a cylindrical object with a poor arm that comes out of it.

478
00:39:57,880 --> 00:40:07,541
And that has four electrodes that basically are shooting lightning bolts back and forth through this waste and heats it up to 1,100 degrees centigrade, where at that

479
00:40:07,542 --> 00:40:09,180
point the mixture is molten.

480
00:40:10,500 --> 00:40:14,460
The molten glass is poured into the canisters through a special bellows assembly.

481
00:40:16,260 --> 00:40:19,720
All of the work is done with robotics because of radioactivity.

482
00:40:22,700 --> 00:40:26,960
The canisters are specially designed and manufactured for the vitrification process.

483
00:40:27,880 --> 00:40:32,380
They weigh 1,200 pounds, and their sides are 3 eighths of an inch thick.

484
00:40:33,160 --> 00:40:36,560
The canister itself is about 10 feet high. It's about 2 feet in diameter.

485
00:40:37,100 --> 00:40:42,840
It's made out of stainless steel, and we'll be pouring at a rate of about a half a gallon per minute into this canister.

486
00:40:42,960 --> 00:40:45,320
It takes roughly 24 hours to fill a canister.

487
00:40:46,780 --> 00:40:49,960
The canisters were rigorously tested before they were approved.

488
00:40:50,920 --> 00:40:53,020
Especially important were the drop tests.

489
00:40:53,021 --> 00:40:57,660
Because the canisters will be transported on public roads,

490
00:40:58,080 --> 00:41:01,880
they must be capable of withstanding any impact that might occur if there were an accident.

491
00:41:04,820 --> 00:41:07,380
It was drop tested from a height of 7 meters.

492
00:41:07,920 --> 00:41:11,380
It was drop tested right side up and right side down,

493
00:41:11,780 --> 00:41:15,960
and it had to survive that drop test without any damage that it would affect its function,

494
00:41:16,080 --> 00:41:17,220
and it's done that successfully.

495
00:41:18,220 --> 00:41:24,280
For decades, scientists have sought an effective way to store nuclear waste for long periods of time.

496
00:41:25,280 --> 00:41:32,820
They looked at many options, from burying it in the ocean, to placing it in polar ice, to sending it to outer space.

497
00:41:34,700 --> 00:41:38,820
The concept of vitrification was selected as the best,

498
00:41:39,400 --> 00:41:46,460
and the main factors that got into that was the incredible stability of the glass form for thousands and thousands and thousands of years,

499
00:41:46,461 --> 00:41:50,640
the availability of melter technology for this application,

500
00:41:51,200 --> 00:41:54,860
and those were probably the dominant factors that caused it to be chosen.

501
00:41:56,660 --> 00:42:01,000
Although strong, the canister itself doesn't prevent the radioactivity from escaping.

502
00:42:02,140 --> 00:42:05,480
We use it as a vessel to capture the glass and let it set up,

503
00:42:05,900 --> 00:42:11,200
and we use it to support transporting the material from here to storage in a federal facility.

504
00:42:11,480 --> 00:42:15,200
So we had to have a can that was about the size that could move on public highways.

505
00:42:16,460 --> 00:42:19,880
Once the molten glass is poured, it is allowed to cool.

506
00:42:21,020 --> 00:42:23,660
Then the outside of the canister is decontaminated.

507
00:42:25,080 --> 00:42:27,140
That is like a sandblasting process.

508
00:42:27,300 --> 00:42:32,160
We'll put it in a cell and spray it with nozzles that spray water and sand.

509
00:42:32,780 --> 00:42:38,120
And we will knock all of the corrosion and the surface contamination off the canister.

510
00:42:40,440 --> 00:42:45,060
Although the waste has now been vitrified, radiation still emanates from it.

511
00:42:46,460 --> 00:42:51,620
So it must still be handled robotically, and no one can approach the canister without being shielded.

512
00:42:52,440 --> 00:42:58,160
The radioactive materials will eventually become harmless, but only after thousands of years of decay.

513
00:42:59,660 --> 00:43:02,880
The canister is sealed by welding a plug into its top.

514
00:43:04,140 --> 00:43:09,740
We use over 40 tons of pressure and a quarter of a million amps of electric current

515
00:43:09,741 --> 00:43:13,600
to place a welded plug in that canister in less than a second and a half.

516
00:43:13,601 --> 00:43:17,420
That's a lot of energy in a small place in a short time.

517
00:43:18,380 --> 00:43:26,920
That process produces a very reliable well that's robust and has been tested and proven for thousands of canisters.

518
00:43:28,540 --> 00:43:32,960
After being welded shut, the canister is transported to a storage area.

519
00:43:33,100 --> 00:43:39,360
We have a special vehicle that transports the canister from our shielded building to the shielded storage areas.

520
00:43:39,820 --> 00:43:41,180
It's a large vehicle.

521
00:43:41,900 --> 00:43:44,560
The diameter of the wheels is about 4 1⁄2 feet.

522
00:43:45,080 --> 00:43:49,480
An operator positions hydraulically the shielded cylinder and the cranes

523
00:43:49,481 --> 00:43:52,360
to both lift the canister up into the vehicle,

524
00:43:52,880 --> 00:43:55,180
and then when the vehicle reaches its final destination,

525
00:43:55,700 --> 00:43:59,800
to remove the shielded plugs and place the canister in its assigned storage location.

526
00:44:00,900 --> 00:44:04,380
The storage area at the Savannah River site is only temporary.

527
00:44:04,381 --> 00:44:11,900
The government's plan is to someday transfer the canisters to a permanent federal repository for high-level waste.

528
00:44:12,840 --> 00:44:15,020
Probably at Yucca Mountain, Nevada.

529
00:44:16,540 --> 00:44:20,420
Whether it's storing nuclear waste or grandma's peaches,

530
00:44:21,020 --> 00:44:24,160
helping to feed pioneers or soldiers,

531
00:44:25,600 --> 00:44:28,660
canning is an essential but unsung part of modern life.

532
00:44:28,661 --> 00:44:36,900
Dependable and recyclable, it relies on a simple little invention that changed our world.

